From: HomeBrace GmbH <info@homebrace.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: imx: drop redundant per-transfer clk_get_rate()

i2c_imx_start() calls clk_get_rate() on every transfer, which takes the
global clk framework prepare_lock. That reintroduces the exact lock edge
removed upstream by commit 90ad2cbe88c2 ("i2c: imx: use clk notifier for
rate changes"), whose whole point was to stop taking prepare_lock per
transfer.

The caching infrastructure from that commit is already present here and
fully functional: i2c_imx->ifdr holds the divider, it is computed once in
i2c_imx_probe(), and i2c_imx_clk_notifier_call() recomputes it whenever
the clock rate actually changes. i2c_imx_start() then writes the cached
i2c_imx->ifdr regardless, so the recomputation is pure overhead — plus a
lock acquisition that deadlocks.

Taking prepare_lock inside an i2c transfer creates an ABBA cycle with any
i2c-attached clock provider, which registers its clock while holding
prepare_lock and then reaches for i2c. On phyCORE-i.MX8M Plus this is hit
by the on-module RV-3028 RTC, and lockdep reports:

  Chain exists of:
    rtc_rv3028:944:(&regmap_config)->lock --> i2c_register_adapter --> prepare_lock

         CPU0                    CPU1
         ----                    ----
    lock(prepare_lock);
                                 lock(i2c_register_adapter);
                                 lock(prepare_lock);
    lock(rtc_rv3028:944:(&regmap_config)->lock);

                  *** DEADLOCK ***

  rv3028_probe -> devm_clk_register -> __clk_register [holds prepare_lock]
               -> rv3028_clkout_recalc_rate -> regmap_read [wants regmap lock]

  <any i2c regmap op> [holds regmap lock] -> i2c_transfer -> i2c_imx_start
               -> clk_get_rate -> clk_prepare_lock [wants prepare_lock]

The window is opened by the PCA9450 PMIC probing asynchronously
(async_run_entry_fn: regulator_set_voltage -> regmap -> i2c ->
clk_get_rate -> prepare_lock) while the deferred-probe worker is inside
rv3028_probe. The result is an intermittent silent boot hang: the probe
workers deadlock while the rest of the kernel keeps running.

This is the same class of bug described in "imx i2c vs clk framework
deadlock fix" (Lucas Stach, 2017, never merged) and fixed for the sibling
controller by "i2c: lpi2c: cache peripheral clock rate".

Restore the upstream behaviour by dropping the redundant call. The
notifier keeps ifdr current, so there is no functional change beyond not
taking prepare_lock on every transfer. This fixes every i2c-attached
clock provider on the SoC, not just the RTC.

Signed-off-by: HomeBrace GmbH <info@homebrace.com>
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c | 4 ----
 1 file changed, 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-imx.c
@@ -722,10 +722,6 @@ static int i2c_imx_start(struct imx_i2c_struct *i2c_imx, bool atomic)
 	unsigned int temp = 0;
 	int result;

-	result = i2c_imx_set_clk(i2c_imx, clk_get_rate(i2c_imx->clk));
-	if (result)
-		return result;
-
 	imx_i2c_write_reg(i2c_imx->ifdr, i2c_imx, IMX_I2C_IFDR);
 	/* Enable I2C controller */
 	imx_i2c_write_reg(i2c_imx->hwdata->i2sr_clr_opcode, i2c_imx, IMX_I2C_I2SR);
